🧱 Core Components of Eureka (Simple Explanation)
Eureka has two main parts:

1. Eureka Server 🖥️
Think of this as a "phone book" or directory service in your application.

It keeps a list of all microservices running in your system.

Services can register themselves here.

Other services can ask it: “Where can I find service X?”

📌 Imagine you're building a food delivery app:

Your User Service, Order Service, and Restaurant Service all register themselves with the Eureka Server.

Eureka Server keeps track:
“Order Service is running at IP A”
“User Service is running at IP B” and so on.

2. Eureka Client 📱
This is every microservice in your app.

Each service is a client of Eureka.

When a service starts, it says:

"Hey Eureka, I'm the User Service — here's where I live!"

Also, when one service wants to talk to another, it asks Eureka:

“Where is Order Service?”

📌 Example:

User Service wants to call Order Service.

Instead of hardcoding the IP or URL, it asks Eureka:
“Give me an instance of Order Service.”

Eureka replies:
“Order Service is running at http://10.0.0.2:8080.”

🔄 Why this is useful?
No need to hardcode URLs/IPs of other services

Services can scale (e.g., 3 instances of the same service) — Eureka will return one randomly (load balancing)

Dynamic communication between services

🔁 Summary in 1 Line:
Eureka Server is the registry, and Eureka Clients are your microservices that register and discover each other through the server.

